1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,104
Three eleven in the morning. The worst storm of the year.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,932
And then my lamp, the lamp I've kept for thirty years, went dark.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,281
Twelve seconds. Just twelve seconds of dark.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,991
Then it came back on. All by itself.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,648
By morning, a boat lay broken on the north rocks.

6
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:42,631
Nobody was hurt, thank the tides. But the harbour master was furious.

7
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:47,505
He said I fell asleep. He said I let the lamp go out.

8
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:53,190
I have three days to prove it wasn't me, or I lose the lighthouse.

9
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,173
Lucky for me, I don't work alone.

10
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:05,230
First stop: the lamp room clock.

11
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:10,321
It runs twelve seconds slow. But nobody has touched it in years.

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,178
Someone moved the pendulum. And look what they left behind.

13
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,545
Wet footprints. Webbed, like a bird's.

14
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,698
But Pip's foot is tiny next to them, and they're longer than my boot.

15
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:31,785
No claws. A flat, straight edge at the back. And they come from the sea window.

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,918
So who made them? Pause now, and think.

17
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,715
Time's up. Those aren't feet at all. They're swim fins.

18
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,991
Someone climbed up from the sea, in the middle of a storm.

19
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:57,965
Someone who didn't want to be seen.

20
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,923
So that night, we kept watch.

21
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:09,345
Pip took the window. Mabel took a nap. Of course.

22
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,191
And Rollo took care of the stake-out sandwiches. All of them.

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,375
Then, at three twelve exactly...

24
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,702
It happened again. Right under our noses.

25
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,195
But this time, I found how.

26
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,360
Three cables feed the lamp. One of them was cut clean through.

27
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:49,519
The cutter had two ways out: the window, or the stairs.

28
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:56,808
Look at the window. A spider's web stretches right across it. Not one thread broken.

29
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:00,388
And on the cut cable, a smudge of yellow.

30
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:04,853
Window, or stairs? Which way did the cutter go?

31
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:19,172
The stairs! The web says nobody went through that window.

32
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:25,760
And the yellow? I painted these stairs last week. The paint never quite dried.

33
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:31,717
Yellow prints. Down the stairs, past the kitchen, all the way

34
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,087
to the cellar door. A door I have never been able to open.

35
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:47,715
It has a lock with four stones. A net, a moon, a sun, and a wave.

36
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:53,693
Above it, someone carved: tell the shadow's story, first to last.

37
00:03:55,200 --> 00:04:00,667
If you heard our Old Tale, The Man Who Sold His Shadow, you already know this one.

38
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:04,654
What order opens the door?

39
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:21,435
The net: forty days of empty nets. The moon: the night the mist woman rose.

40
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:27,475
The sun: noon, and no shadow. The wave: he followed it into the sea.

41
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:34,139
Behind the door, a tunnel. Straight down to the sea.

42
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:39,838
In the wet sand, two sets of prints. Swim fins, and boots.

43
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,065
And on the wall, in my lantern light, I counted our shadows.

44
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:48,676
Four of us. Five shadows.

45
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,530
The next day at noon, with the sun high and bright,

46
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,814
my lamp went dark again.

47
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:04,146
And someone was watching.
